            <ArticleTitle>A biomass approach to mendable bio-elastomers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1306-1313</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c6sm02003h</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Sustainable bioelastomers with high elastic recovery, high resilience and mendability are conceptualized with low chain-entanglement polymers that are predominantly originated from renewable biomass. Polymers with plant oil-derived fatty groups at the side chain were installed with furan, which allowed Diels-Alder addition to introduce dynamic covalent crosslinking. These elastomers are mendable via retro Diels-Alder. Reprocessing of these polymers led to the formation of elastomers with preservation of excellent resilience and elastic recovery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
